# Hostile audit — all-regime wrapping / two-half-disk dichotomy

**Date:** 2026-09-12  
**Reviewed:** `research/target_c_allregime_halfdisk_dichotomy_2026-09-12.md`  
**Disposition:** **PASS at proof-only Type-II scope.**  
**LEVEL I STATUS:** **STILL OPEN. C-II REMAINS OPEN.**

## 1. Dependency and scope check

The candidate needs only the current Type-II cell-tiling facts:

1. normalized force hulls converge to `B_{1/2}`;
2. harmonic attachment cells have pairwise disjoint interiors;
3. their total area is `(pi/4-o(1))D^2`;
4. `sum sqrt(area(P_r))=O(D)`;
5. the total local non-rhombic area deficit is `o(D^2)`.

These are available from the canonical Sections 80/83 chain.  No old `x_B=o(D)`
theorem, cap-minimal equality, common diameter cap, or Section-101 endpoint is used.
The historical report `target_c_fullcore_two_semicircle_groups_2026-09-09.md` therefore
is not a premise.

**Verdict:** dependency reduction is legitimate.

## 2. Closed-semicircle classification at diameter one

For a positive-area limit `Q=conv(S)` with `S` on the radius-`1/2` circle, if
`0 notin int(Q)`, the separating/supporting-line theorem gives a line through the
origin with `Q` in one closed half-plane.  Hence `S` lies in one closed semicircle and
has a minimal support arc of length `alpha<=pi`.

This correctly extends the Section-88 cap language to possible normalized diameter-one
limits.  No claim is made that such a support lies in an **open** semicircle.

If instead `0 in int(Q)`, the candidate stops and records the center-wrapping branch.
Thus the dichotomy is exhaustive even when `diam(Q)=1`.

**Verdict:** PASS.

## 3. Endpoint laminarity

For two closed-cap supports of lengths at most `pi`, partial overlap without containment
makes their four extreme endpoints alternate on the circle.  Their base chords cross
properly.  Each chord is a supporting edge of the corresponding positive-area convex
cell, with the cell on the circular-cap side.  Near the proper chord crossing the two
supported inward half-neighborhoods overlap, contradicting interior disjointness.

The `alpha=pi` endpoint introduces two special possibilities:

- complementary semicircles: arc interiors are disjoint and are allowed;
- equal semicircles: both cells would use the same diameter as a supporting edge on the
  same side, so their inward neighborhoods overlap along the relative interior of the
  diameter.  Distinct positive-area cells therefore cannot have equal minimal
  semicircle supports.

Thus inclusion-maximal retained support arcs have pairwise disjoint interiors and

\[
\sum_i alpha_i<=2pi.
\]

**Verdict:** PASS.  The equal-arc exclusion is necessary and is now explicit in the
candidate.

## 4. Cutoff tail and long-support cells

A possible failure mode is a discarded cell of tiny area but nearly semicircular
support.  The candidate does not assume such a cell has a small support arc.  Instead,
for fixed `eta`, it applies the laminar/cap argument only to cells with area at least
`eta D^2`; all omitted cells are paid by

\[
\sum_{area(P_r)<eta D^2} area(P_r)
\le
\sqrt{eta}D\sum_r\sqrt{area(P_r)}
=O(\sqrt{eta})D^2.
\]

Hence long support in the discarded family does not invalidate the retained cap-area
upper bound.

**Verdict:** PASS.

## 5. Equality classification

For inclusion-maximal retained arcs,

\[
G(alpha)={alpha-\sin alpha\over8},
\qquad
\sum alpha_i<=2pi.
\]

Retained area saturation gives

\[
{pi\over4}-O(\sqrt{eta})
\le\sum_iG(alpha_i)
={1\over8}\sum_i alpha_i-{1\over8}\sum_i\sin alpha_i
\le{pi\over4}.
\]

Both nonnegative deficits therefore vanish with the cutoff:

\[
2pi-\sum_i alpha_i=O(\sqrt{eta}),
\qquad
\sum_i\sin alpha_i=O(\sqrt{eta}).
\]

Fix `epsilon in (0,pi/3)`.

- Intermediate arcs `epsilon<=alpha<=pi-epsilon` are excluded for small cutoff by the
  positive `sin epsilon` cost.
- Small arcs have total cap area at most `pi epsilon^2/24` because
  `alpha-sin alpha<=alpha^3/6`.
- At most two arcs exceed `pi-epsilon` by the `2pi` angular budget.

The retained area tends to `pi/4`, while one semicircle contributes at most `pi/8`.
Therefore exactly two long arcs are necessary; each tends to `pi`.  Disjoint interiors
force complementary limiting semicircles.

**Verdict:** PASS.

## 6. Order of limits and existence of actual defining cells

The source no longer claims that a fixed positive cutoff produces exact semicircles.
It chooses `eta_j->0`, then chooses a sufficiently late finite source for each `eta_j`.
The two long maximal retained arcs at stage `j` belong to actual harmonic cells.  A
circle compactness subsequence makes their support spans tend to complementary
semicircles.

The extreme attachment chord of a span `alpha_j` has normalized length

\[
\sin(alpha_j/2)+o(1)\to1.
\]

Thus the two selected **actual finite-source cell sequences** satisfy `d_i/D->1`.
Harmonic trapping `d_i<=delta_i+2` gives `delta_i/D->1` as well.

This is a valid finite diagonal, not an unsupported passage from a countable limiting
family to one exact maximal cell.

**Verdict:** PASS.

## 7. Rhombus-area transfer

The global cell deficit

\[
\sum_r(area(P_r)-A_{4,r})=o(D^2)
\]

has nonnegative summands.  Each dominant cap group has cell area
`(pi/8-o(1))D^2`; restricting the nonnegative global deficit to that group preserves an
`o(D^2)` upper bound.  Hence each group has strict-rhombus area
`(pi/8-o(1))D^2`.

No assertion is made that the single diameter-scale defining cell contains all that
area.

**Verdict:** PASS.

## 8. Quantifiers and source retention

- The theorem is subsequential, as required for excluding an asymptotic Type-II
  countersequence: every Type-II subsequence has a further subsequence in one of the
  two alternatives.
- Contact-isolated source points are neither removed nor inserted into a core rigidity
  theorem.
- No competitor is claimed.
- No exact triangle is inferred from a limiting semicircle.
- A center-wrapping diameter-one limit is left as a separate near-global carrier case;
  it is not incorrectly fed into a `<1` wrapping theorem.

## Final verdict

**PASS at proof-only Type-II scope.**  The theorem gives a genuine all-regime structural
reduction: arbitrary harmonic-cell geometry is replaced by a positive-area
center-wrapping carrier or two complementary rhombic half-disk groups with two actual
diameter-scale defining components.  Neither branch is yet closed, so C-II remains
open.
